> API and interpretation

Trace decoding requires exactly the requested complete word count and checks the
entire input bit bound, including unread tails. Partial attempted words and
missing complete words both reject.

Boolean vectors are logical streams, not packed-byte files. Complete model/count
metadata are external. Checked shared Golomb parsing rejects truncation and
count overflows and reports only the consumed prefix. The expected length study
explicitly normalizes the finite original support.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Golomb, Run-Length Encodings (1966)|https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1966.1053907]
- [Rice, Some Practical Universal Noiseless Coding Techniques (1979)|https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19790014634]

